 The Euclidean Distance Field (EDF) is a spatial indexing structure used for space-skipping ray traversal in dense voxel volumes. It accelerates rendering by allowing rays to jump over empty space based on precomputed distance values, reducing the number of intersection tests. It differs from sibling structures like [EdgeMap Data Layout](1109-edgemap-data-layout.md) by storing scalar distance magnitudes rather than boundary flags, and from [Gradient Computation via Finite Differences](1077-gradient-computation-via-finite-differences.md) by providing traversal acceleration rather than surface normal estimation.
 
+### [Grid Resolution Heuristic](4871-grid-resolution-heuristic.md)
+The Grid Resolution Heuristic is a method for determining the optimal spatial resolution (number of cells) for leaf-level uniform grids in hierarchical acceleration structures, specifically to minimize the expected traversal cost for ray tracing. It differs from static resolution choices by using the Surface Area Metric to balance the cost of grid traversal against the cost of primitive intersection, adapting the grid density ($\lambda$) to the specific scene geometry and hardware characteristics. Unlike sibling methods that focus on data layout or gradient computation, this heuristic directly informs the structural parameters of the grid itself.
